  "material_conclusion": "CPI release days had higher median path drawdown than same-window weekday controls in both Design and Validation, but the loss-avoidance premise did not generalize. Design CPI days had higher median return and a lower negative-return rate than controls, while Validation showed only a small adverse return gap with Fisher p=0.50074322 and one of two annual direction wins. A complete-day cash veto therefore lacks independent predictive support and would expose the strategy to unmeasured foregone-upside and transition-cost opportunity cost.",
  "uncertainty": [
    "The complete UTC day is a coarse window around the 08:30 Eastern release and does not causally isolate CPI from other information arriving that day.",
    "The frozen design intentionally excludes surprise sign, magnitude, consensus forecasts, core versus headline and intraday timing; those are different future hypotheses, not rescue variants for this family.",
    "No matched-capital economics, post-2024 observation, independent OOS, candidate or forward evidence was opened."
  ],
  "next_action": "EXCLUDE_THIS_EXACT_BLS_CPI_FULL_UTC_DAY_RISK_VETO_FINGERPRINT_AND_RANK_THE_NEXT_INDEPENDENT_OPEN_FAMILY",
  "concept_to_teach": "Higher event-day drawdown is not enough to justify going to cash. A useful risk veto also needs repeatable avoided losses large enough to pay transaction costs and foregone positive returns across years.",
